  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "meta": {
    "id": "art_modes-dorian-sound",
    "slug": "modes-dorian-sound",
    "title": "Dorian: Minor Without the Ache",
    "summary": "Dorian is A natural minor with the b6 raised to a natural 6, which removes the one note that fought the chord and gives the mode its signature i–IV vamp, a major chord over a minor tonic.",
    "tags": ["modes", "ear", "theory"],
    "readingTimeMin": 3,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-15"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The parallel road first, as this pathway always does: A Dorian is A natural minor with the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " raised to a natural "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " — "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " becomes "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ". "
        },
        {
          "text": "A B C D E F# G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", degrees "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 2 b3 4 5 6 b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". One degree changes. Everything else holds still."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The relative road, named once and put down: its parent major is "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". "
        },
        {
          "text": "Two Roads to the Same Mode",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-two-roads"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already made the case for finding the notes that way and then putting it down."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The characteristic note is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " ("
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ")."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The gap is the whole sound. In natural minor, the step above the "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is a semitone; in Dorian, it's a whole tone. That one gap is what \"minor without the ache\" means, and it's why Dorian has nothing that fights a static "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". "
        },
        {
          "text": "Where the Half Steps Fall",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-half-steps"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " has the membership count; "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Ache Above the Fifth",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-the-ache"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " has the avoid-note half."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "scale-compare",
      "props": {
        "root": "A",
        "scales": ["minor", "dorian"],
        "drone": true
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Start the drone and play both runs end to end, counting to six on the way up. The sixth note is the only place the two runs disagree — the step you just took from "
        },
        {
          "text": "E",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " was a half step on one card and a whole step on the other."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The tritone falls between the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " — "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " — the two notes that define Dorian against major and against natural minor. "
        },
        {
          "text": "Where the Tritone Lands",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-the-tritone"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " gave the address; it touches the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The vamp is "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", a "
        },
        {
          "text": "i",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "–"
        },
        {
          "text": "IV",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " "
        },
        {
          "text": "A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " in it is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "6",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Here's the mode's signature: a major "
        },
        {
          "text": "IV",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " over a minor tonic. "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Chords A Minor Gives You",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-the-chords-of-the-key"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " harmonised A natural minor and landed "
        },
        {
          "text": "Dm",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " ("
        },
        {
          "text": "D F A",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ") on the "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Raise the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and it becomes "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — one note moves, and a chord you already know as minor turns major."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "progression-player",
      "props": {
        "chords": ["Dm", "D"],
        "bpm": 66,
        "caption": "The iv of A natural minor, then Dorian's IV — one note, F to F#"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The honest half: "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " major also fits inside Ionian and Mixolydian. What rules those out here is the minor home chord, not this one chord's notes. "
        },
        {
          "text": "When the Harmony Moves",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-when-the-harmony-moves"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already said the "
        },
        {
          "text": "D",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " chord is where the "
        },
        {
          "text": "F#",
          "marks": [
            "code",
            {
              "type": "color",
              "tone": "amber"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " lives — that's the fact this vamp is built on."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "progression-player",
      "props": {
        "chords": ["Am", "D"],
        "bpm": 66,
        "caption": "The vamp itself — i to IV, a major chord over a minor tonic"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Dorian and Aeolian are the one adjacent pair on the brightness ladder that Temperley and Tan's listeners didn't reliably tell apart — "
        },
        {
          "text": "Hold One Note and Listen",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-hold-the-drone"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already told you that. Worth adding: those melodies were unaccompanied, with nothing holding home underneath them, and a mode doesn't exist without a tonal centre. That's a fact about what those listeners were asked to do, not a claim that a drone trains anyone's ear — "
        },
        {
          "text": "What \"Brighter\" Actually Means",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-what-brighter-means"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already said to read a non-significant result as data, not difficulty."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The app's own line for this card is \"Minor with a bright 6th — hopeful rather than sad,\" and that's the register to hear it in: a repertoire association, not a psychoacoustic fact. Dorian is one of the four modes the theory literature broadly agrees are common in rock — "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Reference You Already Own",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "modes-ionian-sound"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " already carries that claim with its attribution."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One degree, and the ache is gone — and it is one fret from every "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " your hand already knows. Go and make the move yourself, with the root still ringing on "
        },
        {
          "text": "Drone",
          "link": {
            "kind": "screen",
            "href": "/drone"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": ", before you see it drawn out fret by fret."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
